	dfs = func(r, c int) {
		if r >= 0 && r < rows && c >= 0 && c < cols && image[r][c] == old {
			image[r][c] = color
			for _, d := range [][2]int{{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}} {
				dfs(r+d[0], c+d[1])
			}
		}
	}
	dfs(sr, sc)
	return image
}
```

## Complexity and common mistakes

BFS and DFS handle every vertex and every edge a constant number of times: O(V + E), which on a grid is O(rows × columns).

- Forgetting to record visited vertices loops forever on a graph with cycles.
- Marking in BFS only when dequeuing lets the same vertex be queued many times.
- Adding only one direction of an undirected edge.

## Going further: minimum spanning trees

Another classic graph problem is the minimum spanning tree: connect every node with the lightest possible set of edges. Kruskal’s algorithm sorts the edges from light to heavy and takes each one that joins two different components, skipping any that would close a cycle. The check uses union-find, which gets its own topic later.

