## Merging two sorted arrays: back to front

The task is to merge `nums2` into `nums1`, which has empty slots at its end. Filling from the front would overwrite numbers not yet handled; filling **from the back** with the larger of the two never does:

```python
def merge(nums1, m, nums2, n):
    i, j, k = m - 1, n - 1, m + n - 1
    while j >= 0:
        if i >= 0 and nums1[i] > nums2[j]:
            nums1[k] = nums1[i]
            i -= 1
        else:
            nums1[k] = nums2[j]
            j -= 1
        k -= 1
```

```cpp
void merge(vector<int>& nums1, int m, const vector<int>& nums2, int n) {
    int i = m - 1, j = n - 1, k = m + n - 1;
    while (j >= 0) {
        if (i >= 0 && nums1[i] > nums2[j])
            nums1[k] = nums1[i--];
        else
            nums1[k] = nums2[j--];
        k--;
    }
}
```

```java
static void merge(int[] nums1, int m, int[] nums2, int n) {
    int i = m - 1, j = n - 1, k = m + n - 1;
    while (j >= 0) {
        if (i >= 0 && nums1[i] > nums2[j])
            nums1[k] = nums1[i--];
        else
            nums1[k] = nums2[j--];
        k--;
    }
}
```

```go
func merge(nums1 []int, m int, nums2 []int, n int) {
	i, j, k := m-1, n-1, m+n-1
	for j >= 0 {
		if i >= 0 && nums1[i] > nums2[j] {
			nums1[k] = nums1[i]
			i--
		} else {
			nums1[k] = nums2[j]
			j--
		}
		k--
	}
}
```

## Sorting in Python

In an interview, use the built-in sort. It is Timsort: **stable**, O(n log n) in the worst case:

```python
nums = [3, 1, 2]
sorted(nums)            # a new list [1, 2, 3]; nums is unchanged
nums.sort()             # sorts in place and returns None
words = ['bb', 'a', 'ccc']
words.sort(key=len)     # by length: ['a', 'bb', 'ccc']
```

```cpp
void demo() {
    vector<int> nums = {3, 1, 2};
    vector<int> copy = nums;
